  <h2>What goes on a landscaping estimate</h2>
  <p>
    A landscaping estimate has two jobs. It has to let a homeowner compare you against two other
    bids, and it has to settle the questions that come up once the sod cutter is running. The
    job-specs strip under the header handles the first: lawn and bed area, soil prep, mulch type and
    depth, irrigation work and the plant warranty. Those are the lines where a cheaper bid is
    usually thinner. The line items are grouped by phase for the second.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Site prep and grading:</strong> turf and bed removal, disposal, compost and soil, and a
      fine grade that drains away from the house.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Planting, sod and mulch:</strong> sod on the measured lawn, plants by container size, and
      mulch by the cubic yard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Irrigation, edging and cleanup:</strong> drip conversions, spray-head changes, edging and
      the final walkthrough.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Hardscape and upgrades, such as a paver walk or a smart controller, go in the optional-items
    table. They are priced but outside the total, so the customer can add them without you rewriting
    the estimate.
  </p>

  <h2>How to take off a landscape job</h2>
  <p>
    Break the yard into zones and measure each one. Rectangles are length × width. For curved beds,
    walk the edge with a measuring wheel or take the area from a site plan or aerial tool, and enter
    that number directly. The Site Takeoff sheet splits the zones into lawn and beds and totals the
    turf you will strip.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bulk material is where landscape estimates drift, because it is sold by volume and specified by
    depth. One cubic yard covers 324 square feet at 1 inch deep, so cubic yards = square feet ×
    depth in inches ÷ 324:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Depth</th><th>One cubic yard covers</th><th>1,000 sq ft needs</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 in.</td><td>324 sq ft</td><td>3.1 CY</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2 in.</td><td>162 sq ft</td><td>6.2 CY</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3 in.</td><td>108 sq ft</td><td>9.3 CY</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4 in.</td><td>81 sq ft</td><td>12.3 CY</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Sod is sold by the pallet, and the square footage on a pallet varies by grower, so the pallet
    size is an input. Plants come from the plan: count specimen trees and shrubs, and for massed
    perennials and grasses use area × 144 ÷ spacing². The Plants and Irrigation sheet totals plants
    by container size, turns them into drip emitters, and adds up heads, pipe and tubing from a zone
    list.
  </p>

  <h2>Pricing a landscape install line by line</h2>
  <p>
    Price sod on the measured lawn and keep the cutting waste in your unit price; the takeoff still
    orders the extra. Price plants by container size, because a 5-gallon shrub and a 1-gallon
    perennial are different amounts of material and labor. Build every unit price from cost on the
    Unit Price Builder: material, plus labor hours at your fully loaded rate, plus equipment rental,
    dump fees and delivery, then overhead, then profit as a
    <a href="/blog/construction-markup-vs-margin/">margin on price rather than a markup</a>. A 15%
    markup on cost is only a 13% margin.
  </p>
  <p>
    The completed example is a front-yard renovation in Fort Collins, Colorado, on clay soil: 992
    square feet of new bluegrass sod and 922 square feet of new beds, 1,914 square feet in all. Site
    prep and grading come to $5,107.90, planting, sod and mulch to $5,566.20, and irrigation, edging
    and cleanup to $3,278. That is $13,952.10 in total, or about $7.29 per square foot. Tax shows as
    $0 because the company includes it in its prices. The 40% deposit is $5,580.84, due on
    acceptance to order plants and sod.
  </p>

  <h2>The existing sprinkler system, and other surprises</h2>
  <p>
    Most renovations tie into an old irrigation system you cannot fully see. Heads get sheared by
    the sod cutter, old lines crack when you trench, and a zone that “worked fine” turns out to have
    a leaking valve. Put a stated rate for repairs beyond your scope in the terms, as the example
    does at $85 an hour plus parts, approved first. Buried concrete, rock and debris are the other
    common surprise. Write anything like that up as a
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a> and get it signed before the work is done. For
    small repairs billed by the hour, a <a href="/templates/tm-ticket/">T&amp;M ticket</a> signed on site
    keeps the record. When the job is finished, bill the balance on an
    <a href="/templates/construction-invoice/">invoice</a> that references the estimate number and any
    approved changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Call 811 before you dig. It marks public utilities, not private lines such as sprinklers,
    landscape lighting or gas to a grill, so ask the owner to flag those, and flag every sprinkler
    head before the sod cutter runs.
  </p>

  <h2>Estimate, quote or proposal?</h2>
  <p>
    For most residential installs, this customer-facing estimate is the whole sale: a price, the
    terms and a signature. Many landscapers call the same document a landscaping proposal,
    especially when it goes out with a design drawing. On commercial work bid to a GC or property
    owner against plans and specs, build your cost on the
    <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">construction estimate worksheet</a> and send a
    <a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">bid proposal</a> with inclusions, exclusions and alternates
    instead. If the customer only wants a fixed price for a defined task, a
    <a href="/templates/construction-quote/">construction quote</a> is shorter.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes on landscaping estimates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Guessing mulch and soil instead of working out area × depth ÷ 324.</li>
    <li>
      Leaving out soil prep. It is where a low bid usually saves money, and where first-season
      failures often start.
    </li>
    <li>
      No rate for repairs to the existing sprinkler system, so every broken line is a negotiation.
    </li>
    <li>
      A plant warranty with no conditions. Tie it to working irrigation and a watering schedule.
    </li>
    <li>No valid-until date. Plant, sod and bulk prices change with the season.</li>
  </ul>
